{
    "title": "Coastal Migratory Pelagic Resources of the Gulf of America and Atlantic Region; 2025-2026 Commercial Accountability Measure for the King Mackerel Run-Around Gillnet Fishery",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 30, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-30/pdf/2025-24028.pdf",
    "summary": "Today\u2019s Federal Register brings a mix of updates: the Postal Service is tightening how certain mail pallets are prepared and labeled, fisheries managers are adjusting a king mackerel limit after last year\u2019s overage, and two quick quota swaps will keep summer flounder and bluefish boats working. There\u2019s also a small correction to an Environmental Protection Agency notice.\n\nFor mailers, the Postal Service is revising rules for how \u201cflats\u201d pallets are built and labeled, including when different types of flat mail can be combined on the same pallet headed to regional facilities. The goal is clearer labels and more consistent preparation, which can help printers, lettershops, and logistics teams move mail more smoothly and reduce handling errors. On the flip side, operations managers and software providers may need to update templates, retrain staff, and watch for mislabels during the transition, especially where mixed, barcoded, and non-barcoded pieces share space.\n\nOn the water, federal fisheries managers are applying a standard rule that lowers next season\u2019s king mackerel limit for the southern Gulf gillnet fishery because last season\u2019s catch went slightly over. The cut is relatively small\u2014about 1,200 pounds\u2014and the gillnet season is set to reopen in south Florida waters on January 20, 2026. This approach supports long-term stock health and stability for the broader fleet and seafood supply. Gillnet operators in that area, however, will feel a tighter cap this season, which could pinch revenue if the fish are abundant.\n\nTwo end-of-year quota transfers aim to keep boats fishing without exceeding state limits. North Carolina is sending 125,000 pounds of its 2025 summer flounder allocation to New Jersey so New Jersey vessels can finish the year, while New Jersey is sending part of its 2025 bluefish allocation to North Carolina for the same reason. These swaps benefit buyers and processors by smoothing landings and avoiding abrupt shutdowns. The tradeoff is that the giving state has a bit less cushion, so careful pacing will still be important.\n\nLastly, the Environmental Protection Agency corrected a previous notice about South Carolina\u2019s regional haze plan, removing an agency name that was added by mistake. It\u2019s a housekeeping fix that improves the public record without practical impact.",
